2026-11-14 14:28:22,719 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] ## Case Question Answer
2026-11-14 14:28:22,719 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Case Q1: Perform an Anti-Virus check. Are there any viruses on the computer?**
2026-11-14 14:28:22,720 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Answer**: The antivirus scan is **INCONCLUSIVE**. ClamAV antivirus scanning was executed against the disk image; however, the clamscan utility is unable to open EnCase disk image format (.E01) containers. Instead, it read the disk image file as a single monolithic file rather than extracting and scanning the actual files within the image. Consequently, the files on the recovered system were not scanned by the antivirus engine, and no definitive malware determination could be made through this method.
2026-11-14 14:28:22,721 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] Other forensic analysis techniques employed during the investigation (YARA scanning for known hacking tool signatures, file hash analysis, and executable file inspection) did not reveal confirmed virus or malware signatures on the system. However, multiple known hacking tools were identified, including packet capture utilities (Ethereal, Cain & Abel), credential extraction tools (samdump.dll), and network reconnaissance software (NetStumbler, Nmap). These are legitimate security research tools that can be used maliciously, but their presence alone does not indicate malware infection.
2026-11-14 14:28:22,721 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Recommendation**: To definitively answer whether viruses are present, use antivirus scanning tools capable of operating on forensic disk image formats, or extract the filesystem from the image and scan the individual files using standard antivirus engines.
